  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nA broad 500 mb trough is currently located over the lower Ohio \nValley this afternoon, and will provide the lift needed to trigger a \nfew spotty light rain showers. The greatest risk will be across \nsouthwest Indiana and portions of southern Illinois and western \nKentucky where rain chances peak at 20-40%. A returning \nisolated shower will also be possible on Friday as one final \nvort max moves through, but overall coverage will be less \ncompared to today with only a 20% chance. \n\nBreezy conditions due to a tight pressure gradient along with \ncloud cover across the northeast will help to keep temperatures \nat or above freezing for most locations overnight. However, the \ncoldest airmass of the season arrives on Friday with 850 mb \ntemps plummeting to around -5C. This translates to highs only in\nthe mid to upper 40s, while slight moderation into the lower \n50s is expected by Sunday. As a 1027 mb sfc high pressure builds\nsouth from Manitoba Friday night into Saturday, calm winds and \nclear skies will allow for a widespread hard freeze with low \ntemperatures dipping down into the 20s Saturday and Sunday \nmorning. \n\nAs the aformentioned trough begins to migrate east of the FA early \nnext week, temperatures begin to moderate closer to normal by \nTuesday and Wednesday. Dry weather conditions will also persist as a \ncutoff 500 mb low is currently progged to remain south of the FA \nover the lower Mississippi River Valley. The LREF remains in good \nagreement with moisture struggling to advect north to produce\nmeasurable precipitation.",
